---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PersonTree from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/PersonTree.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import K from "../data/kosina.json";
import people from "../data/people.json";
// Kosina is not one of this archive's eight published surnames, so there is no
// generated family page for them. FIVE OF THE TEN now have a person page all the
// same: publication was changed on 21 September 2026 from surname to DESCENT, and
// Anka Blazevic's children are blood. The five without one are the Slovene male
// line, who married in, and two who may be living.
// Everything here comes from data/kosina.json, which tools/kosina.py builds from
// the GEDCOM and which applies the living rule in the DATA, so a person who may be
// alive carries no date at all, not merely none on the page.
const by = Object.fromEntries(K.people.map((p) => [p.id, p]));

/* 22 September 2026. THE PAGE NEVER DREW THE JOINT.
   It says Anka Blažević married Pavao Kosina, and it lists the descent, but
   a reader could not see from it how this household attaches to the family
   the archive is about. The join is one woman and it is worth drawing:

     Anka Blažević (1893–1953), daughter of Juraj Blažević and Tereza
     Žubrinić — and SISTER of Ljubomir, who is Hedviga's father. So Anka is
     Hedviga's aunt, and the five Kosina children are Hedviga's first
     cousins. Three of them were lost in 1943.

   Drawn with the kit's PersonTree, which six archives already use and which
   puts parents above, the couple in the middle, children below and siblings
   beside — which is exactly the shape this join has. Nothing new was built
   for it. */
const byId = Object.fromEntries(people.map((x) => [x.id, x]));
const ANKA = "@I189@";
const anka = byId[ANKA];
/* /people/<slug> is the PERSON. /who/<slug> is the namesakes page and is keyed
   on the slugified NAME, not on people.json's slug — which is why linking a
   parent there produced /who/juraj-georgius-jure-georgius-george-blazevic and
   a 404 for /who/juraj-georgius-jure-blazevic. Two routes, two slugs, and the
   link checker caught the difference. */
const node = (r) => ({
  name: r.name,
  href: r.slug ? u(`/people/${r.slug}/`) : null,
  dates: [r.born, r.died].filter(Boolean).join("–") || null,
});
const ankaRel = anka ? anka.rel : { parents: [], spouses: [], children: [], siblings: [] };
const kosinaKids = (ankaRel.children || []).map(node);
const ankaSibs = (ankaRel.siblings || []).map(node);
const C = K.counts;
const name = (id) => (by[id] || {}).name || "";
const lost = K.lostIn1943.map((id) => by[id]);
const line = (id) => { const p = by[id]; if (!p) return ""; 
  return p.living ? "" : [p.born, p.died].filter(Boolean).join(" – "); };
const martin = K.people.find((p) => p.name.includes("Martin"));
const pavao = K.people.find((p) => p.name === "Pavao Kosina");
const kids = pavao ? pavao.children.map((c) => by[c.id]).filter(Boolean) : [];
---
<Base title="The Kosina"
  eyebrow={`Senj and Carniola — ${C.generations} generations · ${C.inTree} people from the family tree, ${C.above} read out of the parish registers, and ${C.household} more of the same household`}
  dek={`${C.people} people, from a Carniolan cottage where three infants died in eight years to an autumn in Senj that took three grown Kosina in thirteen months — the family Hedviga's own aunt married into, and for a long time the family this archive could not see.`}>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid three">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{C.people}</div><div class="l">people</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{C.earliest}</div><div class="l">earliest birth — a Kosinar woman, Carniola</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{C.lostIn1943}</div><div class="l">dead in 1943</div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="lede"><strong>This family had no page here until a week ago, and the reason was a
    rule rather than a doubt.</strong> This archive publishes the people of eight surnames, and
    Kosina is not one of the eight — so ten people who are as much a part of this family as anyone
    in it were nowhere on the site. <strong>They existed here only in fragments: a name on a list
    of the Senj dead, a marriage that brought the Boras in, and one line cut at the foot of
    somebody else's gravestone.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>The rule changed on 21 September 2026.</strong> What decides publication now is
    <em>descent</em> and not the spelling of a surname, so everyone drawn on this page has a page
    of their own — apart from two who may still be living, and those two are named and nothing
    more.</p>
    <p><strong>They join this family twice over.</strong> In 1919 <strong>Pavao Kosina</strong>
    married <a href={u("/people/anka-blazevic/")}><strong>Anka Blažević</strong></a>, the daughter
    of <strong>Juraj Blažević</strong> and <strong>Tereza Žubrinić</strong> —
    <strong>Hedviga's own grandparents</strong>. That makes Anka
    <strong>Hedviga's aunt</strong>, her father's sister, and the five Kosina children of that
    marriage <strong>Hedviga's first cousins</strong>.
    <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem">And <strong>Pavao's own mother was Angelika
    Boras</strong> — the woman through whom <a href={u("/boras/")}>the Boras</a> enter this archive
    at all. <strong>So two of this archive's eight families meet in one Senj household</strong>, and
    it is this one.</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">16 November 1841 · Kranj–Šmartin, book page 64</div>
    <h2>And then the Slovene register was opened, and there he was</h2>
    <p class="lede">The 1888 marriage named the parish. This archive stopped at the border,
    because Croatian registers do. <strong>They did not have to: the Nadškofijski arhiv Ljubljana
    publishes its books free on Matricula, and the parish is there under its own name —
    Kranj–Šmartin.</strong></p>
    <blockquote class="note">Baptismal index, Kranj–Šmartin 1832–1870 —<br />
      <strong>104 — Martin Kozina — 64</strong>, under <strong>1841</strong></blockquote>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <tr><th>Born and baptised</th><td><strong>16 November 1841 — the same day.</strong>
        <span class="dek">The tree had «1841» and nothing more.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Where</th><td><strong>Oberfeichting, house 9</strong> — <strong>Zgornje Bitnje</strong>,
        a village west of Kranj.</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">Ehelich / Unehelich</th><td><strong>The tick is in UNEHELICH</strong> —
        <em>born outside marriage</em>. The register has a column for it and the priest used it.</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">Vater</th><td><strong>The column is blank.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Mutter</th><td><strong>«Gertraud Kosina, hüblnn. Tochter»</strong> — a
        <strong><em>Häusler's</em> daughter</strong>, the bottom of the land-holding order.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Godparents</th><td>Lorenz Pizz and Maria Kashar. Baptised by <strong>Blas. Ulzhar,
        Coop.</strong></td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>TWO REGISTERS, TWO COUNTRIES,
      FORTY-SEVEN YEARS, AND THE SAME ABSENCE.</strong><br />
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem"><strong>Kranj, 1841</strong> — <em>Vater</em>:
      blank. <em>Mutter</em>: <strong>Gertraud Kosina</strong>.</span>
      <span style="display:block"><strong>Senj, 1888</strong> — a dash where the groom's father
      belongs. <strong>«Gertruda Kozina, posjednica.»</strong></span>
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem"><strong>The gap in this family tree is not a
      gap in this family tree. It is what the parish priest wrote down, twice, in two languages,
      because there was nothing else to write.</strong></span></div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>And she rose.</strong> In 1841 she is a cottager's
    daughter. By 1888 her son describes her to a Croatian priest as a
    <strong><em>posjednica</em></strong> — a property-owner. <strong>There is no dagger against
    her name in 1888</strong>, which suggests she was still living, in her seventies, on the day
    he married.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">Every other Kozina baptism in that book belongs to one household</h3>
    <p>There are five in the whole volume, and all five were opened.</p>
    <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <thead><tr><th style="width:17%">Baptised</th><th style="width:22%">House</th>
        <th style="width:31%">Father</th><th>Mother</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Anton</strong> 20 Jan 1834</td><td><strong>Oberfeichting 9</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Johann Kosina</strong>, <em>Häusler</em></td><td><strong>Lucia Labornik</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Petrus</strong> 29 Jun 1836</td><td><strong>Oberfeichting 9</strong></td>
          <td>Johann Kosina, <em>Häusler</em></td><td>Lucia Labornik</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>MARTIN</strong> 16 Nov 1841</td><td class="warn"><strong>Oberfeichting 9</strong></td>
          <td class="warn"><strong>— none —</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Gertraud Kosina</strong>, <em>Häuslerstochter</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Franz</strong> 1853</td><td class="dek">not checked</td>
          <td><strong>Johann Kozina</strong>, <em>Häusler</em></td><td><strong>Ursula Sedej</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ana</strong> 1856</td><td class="dek">not checked</td>
          <td>Johann Kozina, <em>Häusler</em></td><td>Ursula Sedej</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>LOOK AT THE HOUSE COLUMN.</strong>
      <strong>Johann the Häusler's two recorded children were born at Oberfeichting no. 9 — and
      five years after the second of them, Gertraud bore Martin in that same house.</strong>
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem">That does not tell us whether she was his
      sister or his daughter. <strong>It does tell us she was living in his household</strong>,
      and it turns «a Kozina family in the same parish» into <strong>one roof</strong>.</span></div>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>One Häusler called Johann, two wives, twenty-two years —
    and in the middle of it one unmarried woman and one child with no father.</strong> Lucia
    disappears after 1836; Ursula is beside the same husband by 1853. <strong>The 1853 and 1856
    entries spell the surname KOZINA with a z</strong>, which is the spelling the Senj priest
    reached for in 1888.</p>
    <p><strong>And what settles it is his marriage.</strong> The marriage register for 1816–1855
    (sig. 00899) has a column headed <em>«Aeltern des Bräutigams»</em> — <strong>the groom's
    parents</strong>. <strong>Find Johann's marriage and you have Gertraud's parents too, if she is
    his sister.</strong> The volume carries no index and runs to seventy-seven pages, so it is a
    scan rather than a lookup, and it is the next thing to do.</p>
    <p><strong>Gertraud is a <em>Häuslerstochter</em></strong> — a Häusler's daughter — and she
    stands godmother on page 33. <strong>Johann is a Häusler.</strong> Father and daughter, or
    brother and sister, <strong>is likely and it is not established</strong>, and they stand in
    <a href={u("/found/")}>the people this tree does not have</a> and nowhere else.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Two corrections, and they are worth keeping.</strong> Page 33 on its
      own read as <strong>«Josef»</strong>; page 18, with the same wife beside him, reads
      <strong>«Johann»</strong>, and page 33 at eight times magnification reads Johann too —
      <strong>one given name, and a household that would have been split in two</strong>. And the
      1836 child, taken from the index as <strong>«Johan»</strong>, is
      <strong>«Petrus»</strong> at the page. <strong>That is the third time in two days that an
      index has been wrong about this family and the register has been right.</strong></div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">And then her own baptism was found, by admitting a mistake</h3>
    <div class="note"><strong>THIS PAGE ELIMINATED HER, AND THE ELIMINATION WAS WRONG.</strong>
      On 21 September the 1814 candidate was opened and struck out because the father's surname —
      <em>«Franz Kosnar, Hüblar»</em> — <strong>ends in a clear «-r»</strong>, so it was read as
      Kosnar and not Kosina. <strong>The letter was read correctly and the conclusion drawn from
      it was wrong.</strong> The «-r» is how <strong>that clerk</strong> writes this family's
      name. He writes it the same way for her brother, and her brother's children are entered
      <strong>KOSINA</strong> by the next clerk in the next volume. <strong>One family, two hands,
      and a surname that changes shape between them.</strong></div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>The marriage register settled it</strong>, because it has
    a column headed <em>«Aeltern des Bräutigams»</em>.</p>
    <blockquote class="note"><strong>1833, 6. Jänner · Obern feichting 9</strong><br />
      <strong>JOHANN KOSINAR</strong>, <em>Häusler</em>, katholisch, <strong>30</strong>, ledig<br />
      × <strong>LUZIA LABORNIK</strong>, house 3, <strong>38</strong>, ledig<br />
      <span class="dek"><em>des Vaters:</em> <strong>Franz Kosinar, Häusler</strong> ·
      <em>der Mutter:</em> <strong>Mina Tom[sh]shan, Häuslerin</strong></span></blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>And the identification does not rest on the surname at
    all.</strong> Johann Kosina, Häusler of Oberfeichting no. 9, had his first child with
    <strong>Lucia Labornik</strong> on <strong>20 January 1834</strong> — <strong>a year and a
    fortnight after this wedding</strong>. The house, the trade, the wife and the date all agree.</p>
    <p><strong>His father is FRANZ, a Häusler</strong>, with a wife written <em>Tom[sh]shan</em> —
    against the 1814 baptism's <strong>Franz Kosinar, Hüblar</strong> and <strong>Maria
    Do[m]shan</strong>. <em>Mina</em> is the short form these registers use for <em>Marija</em>.
    <strong>The same couple.</strong></p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">So the line runs two generations further back</h3>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th class="warn">Franz Kosinar<br /><span class="dek">× Maria «Mina» Dortschen</span></th>
        <td><strong>Martin's grandparents — and four generations above Hedviga.</strong>
        <span class="dek">Named in six entries across thirty-three years and never once in person:
        their own marriage in 1800, four of their children's baptisms, and their son's marriage in
        1833. He has a death; she does not.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Gertraud</th><td>Baptised <strong>18 March 1814</strong>. <strong>Twenty-seven</strong>
        when she bore Martin.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Johann</th><td>Born about <strong>1802–03</strong>; married <strong>6 January
        1833</strong>; the Häusler of <strong>Oberfeichting no. 9</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Martin</th><td>Born <strong>16 November 1841</strong> at Oberfeichting no. 9,
        <em>unehelich</em> — <strong>in his uncle's house</strong>.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>Gertraud was not a lodger and not a stranger. She was the
    householder's sister</strong>, and she bore her son under her brother's roof seven years after
    he brought a wife into it.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">And then their own marriage, which explains the houses</h3>
    <p>The 1780–1815 marriage book has no index either, so images 30–35 were cropped to the
    <em>Bräutigam</em> column and read as strips. It is on image 33, under <strong>1800</strong>.</p>
    <blockquote class="note"><strong>Den 27ten [8ber] · Feichting b. St. Joh. · 10</strong><br />
      <strong>FRANZ KOSINAR</strong>, katholisch, <strong>24</strong>, ledig<br />
      × <strong>MARIA DORTSHEN</strong>, katholisch, <strong>20</strong>, ledig<br />
      <span class="dek"><em>Beystände:</em> <strong>MATTHÄUS KOSINAR</strong> — signing with a
      cross — and Simon Rauth, <em>Bauern</em>. Married by Matthäus Lauts, coop.</span></blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>27 October 1800</strong>, at twenty-four and twenty. So
    <strong>Franz was born about 1776 and Maria about 1780</strong>.</p>

    <div class="note"><strong>THE TWO HOUSE NUMBERS, AND A FIRST ANSWER THAT DID NOT SURVIVE.</strong>
      This page had said the numbers were «not tight» — <strong>Gertraud baptised at house 10,
      Johann marrying into house 9</strong> — and on 22 September it explained them as
      <em>«house 10 is the parents', house 9 is the brother's»</em>.
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem"><strong>That was wrong, and the register said
      so the same evening.</strong> Franz's own children are baptised at <strong>house 9 in
      1812</strong>, at <strong>house 10 in 1814 and 1815</strong>, and at
      <strong>Oberfeichting 9 again in 1820</strong> — where the clerk writes him
      <em>Häubler</em>, the man of the house. <strong>Both houses were his at different times</strong>,
      and the family moved between them. The explanation is kept here, struck through, because it
      is a fair example of how quickly a tidy answer can be built on two data points.</span></div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>A third Kosinar — and for one day nobody could say what
    he was.</strong> <strong>MATTHÄUS KOSINAR</strong> stood witness and <strong>signed with a
    cross, because he could not write</strong>. A man of the same surname witnessing for a
    twenty-four-year-old groom is most likely his father or his brother, and this page said for a
    day that <em>nothing here says which</em>.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>His death says which.</strong> Mrliška knjiga 1812–1835, book p. 64:
      <strong>«1819, den 16ten April · Ober-Feichting · 14 · MATTHÄUS KOSINA ·
      <em>Keischler</em> · ALTER 45 · Rothlauf und Brand»</strong>.
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem"><strong>Forty-five in 1819 is born about 1774.
      Franz was born 1775–76.</strong> Two men born a year apart are not father and son —
      <strong>so the man who could not sign his name in 1800 was Franz's brother, and he was
      twenty-six when he stood up for him.</strong></span></div>
    <p><strong>And the 1818 candidate is settled rather than merely moot.</strong> Her unreadable
    father — <em>«Lukas Ko[s]in…»</em>, house 12 — is on image 31 of this same book:
    <strong>«LUKAS KOSINA, katholisch, 29, ledig», married 1799</strong>, so born about 1770. A
    real man and a different household. <strong>The 1814 Gertraud is left with no rival.</strong></p>

    <div class="note"><strong>The mother's surname, from six clerks across thirty-three years.</strong>
      <strong>«Dortshen»</strong> 1800 · <strong>«Dortschen»</strong> 1812 ·
      <strong>«Do[m]shan»</strong> 1814 · <strong>«Dortschen»</strong> 1815 ·
      <strong>«Dortschen»</strong> 1820 · <strong>«Tom[sh]shan»</strong> 1833.
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem"><strong>Four of the six read Dortschen</strong>,
      and the two that do not are this archive's own bracketed readings of a hand it found hard.
      <strong>On 22 September 2026 this page changed its lead spelling to «Dortschen» and kept all
      six.</strong> <em>Mina</em> is the short form these registers use for <em>Marija</em>, and one
      clerk writes <em>Marina</em>.</span></div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">And one rung further up is a wall, for now</h3>
    <p><strong>Franz's own baptism is not in this parish.</strong> He was twenty-four in October
    1800, so born between late 1775 and late 1776. The baptismal register was walked from
    <strong>October 1775 to January 1777</strong> — ten openings, every <em>Infans</em> and
    <em>Parentes</em> column — and <strong>there is no Kosinar in it at all</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>The absence says something</strong>, because the same surname is all over the same
    register fifty years later: Johann's children in 1834, 1836, 1853 and 1856, and Gertraud's
    Martin in 1841. <strong>In the mid-1770s it is simply not there.</strong></p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>He came from elsewhere</th><td>He married <strong>into</strong> Feichting house 10 at
        twenty-four, which is what a man who marries a household does — and it would make the
        Kosinar of Kranj–Šmartin <strong>his descendants rather than his ancestors</strong>.
        <span class="dek">Matthäus the witness cuts against it, and proves nothing either way.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>The age is wrong</th><td><strong>«24» in a marriage register is very often a round
        number.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>The name is unreadable</th><td>The 1770s hand may write the surname unrecognisably —
        <strong>which it already proved to do in 1814</strong>, when «Kosinar» was read as «Kosnar»
        and a real candidate was struck out on this very page.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">So the death register was opened, and it confirmed him</h3>
    <p>The death index is letter-tabbed, and the whole <strong>K</strong> section for 1836–1869
    holds <strong>exactly two Kozina</strong>.</p>
    <blockquote class="note"><strong>22. — 24. [Jänner 1849] · Ob. Feichtg · 9</strong><br />
      <strong>FRANZ KOSINA</strong>, <em>Ausnehmer</em> · katholisch · männlich ·
      <strong>ALTER 73</strong> · <em>Lungenentzündung</em> · versehen</blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Aged 73 in January 1849 — born 1775–76</strong>, which
    <strong>independently confirms the «24» he gave at his own wedding</strong> forty-nine years
    earlier. Two records, two clerks, one birth year. <strong>And it means the missing baptism
    really is missing</strong>, rather than the age being wrong.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>One word in that entry tells you what happened to the house.</strong>
      He is an <strong><em>Ausnehmer</em></strong>: a householder who has <strong>handed
      over</strong> and lives out his life on the <em>Ausnahm</em>, the retirement provision
      written into the transfer.
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem">He died at <strong>Oberfeichting 9</strong> —
      the house <strong>he himself held in 1820</strong>, that <strong>his son Johann was keeping
      by 1833</strong>, and that <strong>his grandson Martin was born in in 1841</strong>.
      <strong>He did not move in with his son. He gave his son the house and stayed in it.</strong></span></div>

    <blockquote class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>30. — 2. Xbr [1850] · Ob. Feichtg · 9</strong><br />
      <strong>LUZIA KOSINA</strong>, <em>Hüblersweib</em> · weiblich · <strong>ALTER 55</strong> ·
      <em>Lungenentzündung</em> · versehen · Jos. Kartely</blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Lucia Labornik.</strong> Aged 55 in November 1850, so born
    about 1795 — and she was <strong>38 at her marriage in January 1833</strong>, which gives
    1794–95. <strong>The same woman.</strong> She is entered a Hüblers-<em>Weib</em> and not a
    widow, which is right, because Johann outlived her.</p>
    <p><strong>And it answers a question this page had left hanging:</strong> why Johann is beside
    <strong>Ursula Sedej</strong> by 1853. <strong>Because Lucia died in 1850</strong> — in the
    same house and of the same pneumonia as his father, twenty-two months apart.</p>

    <div class="note"><strong>MARIA IS NOT THERE.</strong> The K section for 1836–1869 has Franz
      and Luzia <strong>and nobody else</strong>. So the earlier index — <strong>1812–1835</strong>
      — had to be read, and on <strong>22 September 2026</strong> it was, end to end.
      <strong>It did not give her. It gave five other people.</strong></div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>The scans are the archdiocese's, not ours</strong>,
    so they are transcribed here and linked rather than republished — index sig. <span class="mono">04632</span>
    image 246, register sig. <span class="mono">00886</span> image 65, at
    <span class="mono">data.matricula-online.eu</span>. </p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Mrliška knjiga Kranj–Šmartin 1812–1835 · page 34</div>
    <h2>The page we went to find one woman on, and found two</h2>
    <p class="lede">The index for 1812–1835 lists <strong>«Kozina Maria — 34»</strong> under the
    year 1816. Page 34 is the left leaf of the eighteenth opening, and the year is written across
    the top of it. <strong>It carries two Maria Kozinar, four weeks apart, and neither of them is
    Martin's grandmother.</strong></p>

    <blockquote class="note"><strong>Den 27 [Februar 1816] · Z[ubern] · 4</strong><br />
      <strong>MARIA KOZINAR</strong>, <em>Häubl.</em> · katholisch · weiblich ·
      <strong>ALTER 78 Jahre</strong> · <em>Altersschwäche</em></blockquote>
    <blockquote class="note" style="margin-top:.9rem"><strong>Den 31 [März 1816] · Oberfeichting · 9</strong><br />
      <strong>MARIA KOZINAR</strong>, <em>Häubl.</em> · katholisch · weiblich ·
      <strong>ALTER 3/4 Jahr</strong> · <em>Fraiß</em></blockquote>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>A woman of seventy-eight and a baby of nine months.</strong>
    Maria Dortschen was twenty at her wedding in 1800, so in 1816 she was thirty-six. The first
    woman was born about <strong>1738</strong> and the register says why she died — <em>Altersschwäche</em>,
    the weakness of age. The second had lived three quarters of a year and died of
    <em>Fraiß</em>, convulsions, which is what this book writes over almost every infant.
    <strong>The index was not wrong. It listed both, one line under the other, and the archive had
    only read the first line.</strong></p>

    <div class="note"><strong>And the baby is Maria Dortschen's own daughter.</strong>
      The baptismal index for 1812–1831 has a <strong>Kosina Maria</strong> at page 44 under 1815,
      and page 44 reads: <strong>«den 8ten [August 1815] · Haus 10 · MARIA · ehelich · Vater:
      FRANZ KOSINAR, <em>Häubler</em> · Mutter: MARIA DORTSCHEN»</strong>.
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem">Born 8 August 1815, dead on 31 March 1816.
      <strong>Seven and a half months — which is what three quarters of a year means in that
      column.</strong> The woman we went looking for was not on page 34; <strong>her daughter
      was.</strong></span></div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">So the whole K section was read, and the house came with it</h3>
    <p>Reading one page for one name is how a search gets long. The <strong>K</strong> section of the
    1812–1835 death index runs across two openings and holds <strong>six Kozina</strong>; the
    baptismal index for the same years holds <strong>eight</strong>. Read together they give a
    household.</p>
    <p class="dek"><strong>One word recurs below and it is worth having.</strong> A
    <em>Häubler</em> — the same word as <em>Häusler</em> and written <em>Hüblar</em> by yet another
    clerk — is <strong>a cottager</strong>: a man with a house and a garden and not enough land to
    live off, the bottom rung of the village land-holding order. It is what Franz was, what his son
    Johann was, and what Martin was born into.</p>

    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th style="width:24%">Who</th><th style="width:30%">What the register says</th>
        <th>How they stand to the line</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {(K.household || []).map((h) => (
          <tr class="above">
            <td>{h.slug
                  ? <a href={u(`/found/${h.slug}/`)}><strong>{h.name}</strong></a>
                  : <strong>{h.name}</strong>}
              <span class="tag">parish register · not yet in the family tree</span></td>
            <td class="dek mono">{h.when}</td>
            <td class="dek">{h.what}
              <span style="display:block;margin-top:.35rem"><strong>{h.how}</strong></span></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>One entry does more work than the rest.</strong>
      Valentin was baptised on <strong>15 February 1820</strong> and buried on the <strong>29th</strong>,
      aged one week — 1820 was a leap year, so the date is real. His baptism reads
      <strong>«Ober-Feichting · 9 · VALENTIN · ehelich · Vater: FRANZ KOSINA, <em>Häubler</em> ·
      Mutter: MARINA DORTSCHEN»</strong>.
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem"><strong>It is the only record that puts Franz
      himself at Oberfeichting 9 as the householder</strong> — twenty-one years before his grandson
      Martin was born in that house, and twenty-nine before he died in it as an
      <em>Ausnehmer</em>. The house was never his son's alone. <strong>It was his.</strong></span></div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Three of their five known children did not
      live.</strong> Elisabeth five months, Maria nine months, Valentin fourteen days — 1812, 1815
      and 1820, in one cottage. The two who grew up are <strong>Johann</strong>, born about 1802,
      and <strong>Gertraud</strong>, born in 1814 between two of the deaths, who lived to bear
      Martin. <strong>Five is what is known, not what there were</strong>: three more Kosina
      baptisms of these years have not been opened yet.
      <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem"><strong>That is not a remarkable household. It
      is an ordinary one</strong>, and it is the first time this archive has been able to say so
      about anybody in Carniola.</span></div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">Where Maria Dortschen's death is not</h3>
    <p><strong>The search for it is now exhaustive for this parish</strong>, which is worth more
    than another guess. The <strong>K</strong> section of the death index for
    <strong>1812–1835</strong> holds Elisabeth 1813, two Maria in 1816, Matthäus 1819, Valentin 1820
    and Margareth 1826. The <strong>K</strong> section for <strong>1836–1869</strong> holds Franz
    1849 and Luzia 1850. <strong>She is in neither.</strong></p>
    <p>She was alive in <strong>February 1820</strong>, at about forty, because Valentin was
    baptised then and entered <em>ehelich</em>. So she died <strong>unindexed, after 1869 at
    eighty-nine or more, or in another parish</strong> — and the next place to look is the death
    registers of 1870–1890 and 1890–1911, which are also free.</p>

    <div class="note"><strong>One thing this index got wrong, and it is worth saying.</strong>
      It sends a reader to page <strong>132</strong> for Margareth Kozinar. <strong>She is on page
      130</strong>, one opening earlier, in the house. Every other reference in the section landed
      on the page it promised. <strong>An index that is right fifteen times and wrong once is still
      the fastest way in — but the page number is a hint and not a fact.</strong></div>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>The scans are the archdiocese's, not ours</strong>,
    so they are transcribed here and linked rather than republished. Deaths 1812–1835, sig.
    <span class="mono">00894</span>, images 4, 18, 33, 37 and 66; its index, sig.
    <span class="mono">04634</span>, images 125–126. Baptisms 1812–1831, sig.
    <span class="mono">00885</span>, images 4, 23 and 55; its index, sig.
    <span class="mono">04631</span>, image 169. All at <span class="mono">data.matricula-online.eu</span>.
    <strong>Still unread in the same free books: the baptisms of a Dorothea and a Marianna Kosina,
    both 1813, and a second Maria in 1818.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The name</div>
    <h2>Kosina, or Kozina — and they are not the same word</h2>
    <p class="lede">The family writes <strong>Kosina</strong>, with an s, and has done for over a
    century. <strong>The one document that says where they came from writes it with a z.</strong></p>
    <blockquote class="note">Senj marriages, 12 January 1888, entry 1 — the groom is entered
      <strong>KOZINA</strong>, and his origin is given as <em>Kranjska župa sv. Martin kod
      Krainburg</em>.</blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>That single letter carries the whole question</strong>,
    because the two spellings are two different words.</p>
    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Kozina — goatskin</h3>
        <p>From <strong><em>koza</em></strong>, «goat». The standard dictionary of Slovene, the
        <strong>SSKJ²</strong>, gives <em>kozina</em> two senses and both are the same animal:
        <strong><em>usnje iz kozje kože</em></strong> — leather made from goat hide — and
        <strong><em>kozje meso</em></strong>, goat meat.</p>
        <p><strong>It is an ordinary Slovene noun</strong>, and it is an ordinary Slovene surname:
        the Slovene Wikipedia keeps a page of notable bearers, among them the composer
        <strong>Marjan Kozina</strong> (1907–1966).</p>
        <p class="dek"><strong>This is the family's own spelling in the family's own oldest
        record</strong>, in the hand of the priest who also wrote down that the groom came from
        Carniola.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Kosina — the scythe, or the blackbird</h3>
        <p>With an s it belongs to a different root: <strong><em>kosa</em></strong>, «scythe» —
        so a mower, a harvester, or a maker of scythes — or, on another reading of the same
        syllable, <strong><em>kos</em></strong>, the <strong>blackbird</strong>. The
        <strong>-ina</strong> ending is the ordinary Slavic «belonging to».</p>
        <p>There is also a <strong>village called Kosina</strong> in south-eastern Poland, which
        gives the Polish surname <em>Kosiński</em>, «someone from Kosina».</p>
        <p class="dek"><strong>None of that is this family.</strong> It is worth setting out only
        because it is what a search engine will tell you, and it is the wrong answer for a
        household that came out of a Slovene parish near Kranj.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>So the honest reading is this.</strong> The older,
    Slovene, documented form is <strong>Kozina</strong>, and it means <strong>goatskin</strong> —
    a herdsman's or a tanner's word, from the same hill country the Kranj parishes sit in.
    <strong>Kosina is what the name became after it settled in a Croatian town</strong>, where the
    z softened to an s and nobody was pronouncing it as a Slovene word any more.</p>
    <p><strong>And that is not a footnote — it is why the family could not be found.</strong> Five
    spellings of <em>Kosina</em> had been searched against the Croatian indexes and returned
    nothing. The name was in the other language all along.
    <a href={u("/name/")}>The other names in this archive →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The question everybody asks</div>
    <h2>Is there a Kosina coat of arms? No — and here is how that was checked</h2>
    <p class="lede">Type the name into a search box and several companies will sell you a
    «Kosina family crest» on a scroll. <strong>They are selling a picture attached to a string of
    letters.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Arms are granted to a PERSON</strong>, and descend in that person's line.
    <strong>There has never been such a thing as a coat of arms belonging to everyone who happens
    to share a surname</strong> — no more for Kosina than for Smith. So the real question is
    narrower and answerable: <em>was there ever an armigerous Kozina family in the lands this one
    comes from?</em></p>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <tr><th class="warn">Croatia — nothing</th><td><strong>Ivan von Bojničić, <em>Der Adel von
        Kroatien und Slavonien</em> (Nürnberg, 1899)</strong>, the standard register of Croatian
        and Slavonian noble families and their arms. <strong>The full text was searched on
        21 September 2026: not one Kosina and not one Kozina.</strong> <span class="dek">And the
        search is sound — the same file returns Korjenić 119 times and Kovačić 13.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">Slovenia — nothing</th><td><strong><em>Plemiška heraldika na
        Slovenskem</em></strong>, the ZRC SAZU database of noble arms surviving in Slovenia.
        <strong>No Kozina.</strong></td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>And the family's own documents say why, without any
    apology being needed.</strong> Martin Kozina is entered a <strong><em>trgovac</em></strong> —
    a merchant. His mother is a <strong><em>posjednica</em></strong>, a property-owner, and the
    register puts <strong>a dash where his father belongs</strong>. Pavao's godfather in 1894 was
    <strong>another merchant</strong>. <strong>This is a trading household out of a Carniolan hill
    parish, and the name itself means goatskin.</strong> None of that is armigerous. None of it is
    a lesser thing, either — <strong>it is simply what they were, and it is why the register can
    tell you so much about them.</strong></p>
    <div class="note"><strong>What is real, if you want something to put on a wall.</strong> The
      arms of <strong>KRANJ</strong> — <em>Krainburg</em>, the town named in Martin's marriage
      entry — are <strong>a red eagle with spread wings on a silver field</strong>. And the same
      eagle is the ancient device of <strong>Carniola itself</strong>, the <em>Kranjska</em> that
      the register names in the very cell that told this archive where the family came from.
      <strong>The family has no arms. The place they came out of has a very good one.</strong></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">1857 · Senj marriages, entry 52</div>
    <h2>It begins with a marriage in a front room</h2>
    <p class="lede">Before there is a Kosina here there is a <strong>Boras</strong>, and their own
    beginning is one of the more human entries in this archive.</p>
    <blockquote class="note"><em>Caelebs <strong>Josephus Boras</strong>, filius Denatorum Josephi
    et Annae Boras natae <strong>Butvalz</strong> — cum <strong>Vidua Maria</strong> … in
    <strong>domo privata</strong> copulate fuere.</em><br />
    <span class="dek">Senj marriages, <strong>9 February 1857</strong>, entry 52. Ages 32 and 28.</span></blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">They married <strong>in a private house</strong>, by dispensation
    of the bishop. He is entered a <em>bachelor</em>, she a <strong>widow</strong> — and they
    already had <strong>three children</strong>. It is a <strong>legitimation</strong>: a union of
    some years put right in a front room under episcopal licence.</p>
    <p><strong>Angelika Boras follows the marriage</strong>, later in 1857. She is the one who
    becomes a Kosina, and she died in <strong>1902</strong> — before her son married, and forty-one
    years before the raid.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Six generations</div>
    <h2>The descent</h2>
    <p class="lede"><strong>Two sources, one table</strong> — and the tinted rows are the
    better-evidenced half, not the weaker one.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <strong>What «the family tree» means here, because it is not obvious.</strong>
      <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0"><strong>The family tree is a separate document.</strong> It is
      the one kept on MyHeritage — <strong>15,643 people</strong> — and it is where almost
      everything on this site comes from. <strong>This archive reads it and never writes to
      it.</strong></p>
      <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0">This archive also reads <strong>parish registers</strong>. When
      it finds somebody in a register whom the tree has never heard of, it records them
      <strong>beside</strong> the tree instead of quietly slipping them in — so that you can always
      tell which statement came from a document and which came from the tree.</p>
      <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0"><strong>So «not in the family tree» does not mean «not
      proven».</strong> It means nobody has typed them in yet. <strong>Look at what the two halves
      of this table actually cite.</strong> Martin's own row says <em>«1841»</em> and offers no
      source at all. <strong>Gertraud's gives a day, a house number and the page of the register it
      was read from.</strong> The tinted rows are the ones with documents behind them.</p>
      <p style="margin:.6rem 0 0"><strong>What decides which side a person falls on is not
      evidence. It is only whether somebody has entered them into MyHeritage</strong> — and none of
      the tinted rows has been, yet.</p>
    </div>

    <details style="margin-top:1rem">
      <summary style="cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;color:var(--accent)">
        <strong>To add them to the family tree — what to type, and what to cite</strong></summary>
