  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">12 January 1888 · Senj marriages, page 209, entry 1</div>
    <h2>Where they came from, in one cell of one page</h2>
    <p class="lede">For as long as this archive has held the Kosina, the question above Martin
    has been blank. <strong>His baptism is in no index anywhere in the world</strong> and his
    mother appears in exactly one record. The answer was never going to come from a search box —
    it was in the <em>Locus Originis</em> column of his own marriage.</p>
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/line/4505845-martin-kosina-and-angjelika-boras-marriage-1888.jpg")}
           alt="Senj marriage register, 12 January 1888, Martin Kozina and Angjelika Boras" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Senj marriages, page 209, entry 1 — <em>1888 dne 12 siečnja</em>. The scan had
      been in this archive's own media library, unread, for weeks.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <blockquote class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Kranjska župa sv. Martin kod
      KRAINBURG</strong></blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Kranjska is Carniola.</strong> <strong>Krainburg is the
    German name of Kranj.</strong> So <strong>Martin Kozina was born in the parish of St Martin
    near Kranj — in what is now SLOVENIA</strong>, about 1841, and came down to the Croatian
    coast as a <em>trgovac</em>, a merchant. He was <strong>47</strong> and a bachelor when he
    married; Angjelika Boras of Senj was <strong>30</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>It explains every dead end at once.</strong> No Croatian baptism, because he was
    not baptised in Croatia. The surname spelled <strong>Kozina with a z</strong>, which is the
    Slovene spelling and the reason five spellings of <em>Kosina</em> found nothing. And his
    mother called <strong>Gertruda</strong> — a German name that looks wrong on the Velebit coast
    and is ordinary in a Habsburg crownland whose towns carried German names.</p>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <tr><th>His parents</th><td><strong>Gertruda Kozina, <em>posjednica</em></strong> — a
        property-owner — <strong>and a dash where the father should be</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Her parents</th><td><strong>Josip Boras, <em>gradski stražar</em></strong>, a city
        watchman, and <strong>Maria <em>rodjena</em> Kabalin</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Witnesses</th><td><em>Mile Crnković, trgovac</em> — and <strong>Ivan Boras, <em>kr.
        pomorske straže nadstojnik</em></strong>, superintendent of the royal maritime guard.
        <strong>The bride's brother</strong>, signing at her wedding</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Banns</th><td>By <strong>dispensation of the Senj Ordinariate</strong>, 7 January
        1888, no. 38 — <strong>read once instead of three</strong>, as at the Boras marriage of
        1857</td></tr>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">1919</div>
    <h2>How this household joins the Blažević line</h2>
    <p class="dek">One marriage carries it, and it is worth drawing rather than
    describing. <strong>Anka Blažević was Ljubomir's sister</strong> — both children of
    Juraj Blažević and Tereza Žubrinić — so she was <strong>Hedviga's aunt</strong>, and the
    five Kosina children below were <strong>Hedviga's first cousins</strong>.
    Three of them did not survive 1943.</p>
    {anka && (
      <div style="margin-top:1.2rem">
        <PersonTree
          self={node(anka)}
          parents={(ankaRel.parents || []).map(node)}
          spouses={(ankaRel.spouses || []).map(node)}
          siblings={ankaSibs}
          childGroups={kosinaKids.length ? [{ label: `${kosinaKids.length} Kosina children`, rows: kosinaKids }] : []}
          always={true}
        />
      </div>
    )}
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Ljubomir is on
    <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>the direct line</a>; Hedviga is the generation below him.
    Everyone drawn here who has a page of their own is linked.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Anka Blažević marries Pavao Kosina</h2>
    <p class="lede">On <strong>20 November 1919</strong>, at Senj. She was a
    <a href={u("/senj/")}>tobacco-factory</a> worker, as her sister-in-law
    <a href={u("/milka/")}>Milka</a> was. She was born <strong>7 October 1893</strong>, the daughter
    of Juraj Blažević and Tereza Žubrinić, and <strong>the sister of Hedviga's father</strong>.</p>
    <p>Five children followed in the next five years, and then a long gap, and then one more:
    {kids.map((k, i) => (<span>{i ? ", " : ""}<strong>{k.name.replace(" Kosina", "")}</strong>{
      k.living ? "" : ` (${[k.born, k.died].filter(Boolean).join(" – ")})`}</span>))}.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">15 March 1894 · Senj births, page 223, entry 37</div>
    <h2>The parish wrote his death in the margin of his birth</h2>
    <p class="lede">The fourth Kosina scan had been in this archive's own library since
    <strong>9 September</strong> and had never been opened. It is the most complete single
    document this family has.</p>
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/line/4505826-pavao-kosina-birth-1894.jpg")}
           alt="Senj baptismal register, page 223, entry 37 — Pavao Kosina, 1894" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Senj births, page 223. Entry 37 is the sixth row down.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <tr><th>Born</th><td><strong>1894, ožujak 15</strong> — 15 March, as the tree has it.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Baptised</th><td><strong>travanj 16</strong> — <strong>16 April 1894</strong>, a
        month and a day later. <span class="dek">Most children on this page were christened
        within a fortnight.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Name</th><td><strong>Pavao</strong> — and above it, in a later hand,
        <strong>† 8. X. 1943.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Parents</th><td><em>zakonit</em> — legitimate. <strong>Martin Kosina i Angjelika
        r. Boras</strong>, of <strong>Senj</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Godparents</th><td><strong>Mihael Bosco, <em>trgovac</em>, i Franjo Berčić.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Baptised by</th><td><strong>Vinko Margan, kapelan.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Margin</th><td><em>«vjenčan dne <strong>20. XI. 1919</strong>»</em></td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>The death is the thing.</strong> Until today the only
    authority for <strong>8 October 1943</strong> was <strong>the stone his wife and sons
    raised</strong> — and this archive's own rule is that <em>a gravestone is a memorial, not a
    register</em>. <strong>Now the register says it too</strong>, written above his name in the
    book that recorded his birth forty-nine years earlier. <strong>The two agree to the
    day.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>And the marriage, for the third time.</strong> A reading, an index and an
    annotation — three separate books — all give <strong>20 November 1919</strong>. The bride in
    the margin is written <em>«Antom»</em> or <em>«Ankom»</em>, one letter in a hand that appears
    twice on the page and nowhere else. <strong>The person is not in doubt; the letter is.</strong></p>
    <p class="dek"><strong>The godfather was another merchant.</strong> Martin Kosina was a
    <em>trgovac</em> and a Slovene incomer; <strong>Bosco is an Italian surname</strong>. That is
    Senj's commercial class in a single line, and it is where to look if this family is ever
    traced sideways instead of upwards.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">1940–1943 · thirteen months</div>
    <h2>What happened to this household</h2>
    <p class="lede">Read the dates in order and the page stops being a genealogy.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>22 January 1941</th><td><strong>Križan</strong>, born the previous May, dies at
        <strong>eight months old</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>7 September 1943</th><td><strong>Neda</strong> dies, aged nineteen. Her stone
        says what of: <strong><em>žrtva zračnog napada</em></strong> — <strong>a victim of an air
        attack</strong>. A month before the October raid, so <strong>Senj was bombed more than
        once</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>7–8 October 1943</th><td>German aircraft bomb Senj, killing <strong>thirty local
        residents</strong> and twenty-two others and destroying <strong>about half the town</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>8 October 1943</th><td><strong>Pavao Kosina is killed</strong>, on the second day.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>11 November 1943</th><td><strong>Otilija</strong> dies, aged twenty-three —
        <strong>five weeks after her father</strong>, and her stone does <strong>not</strong> call
        her a victim of anything. She was <strong><em>čin. Velike župe</em></strong>, a clerk of
        the wartime administrative region.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>Three of a household of seven, inside thirteen months.</strong>
    Anka outlived them all by a decade and died in <strong>1953</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Senj cemetery · the second Kosina stone</div>
    <h2>OTILIJA i NEDA</h2>
    <p class="lede">The two daughters have a wall plaque of their own, a few steps from their
    father's. <strong>It is the document that gave this page every date in the table above</strong>,
    and it arrived from the family on 21 September 2026.</p>
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/line/9000003-otilija-and-neda-kosina-gravestone-senj.jpg")}
           alt="The marble plaque of Otilija and Neda Kosina in the Senj cemetery" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Senj cemetery. Read here at four times magnification, one line at a time —
      which is how the day of Otilija's death turned out to be the eleventh and not the
      fourteenth.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <blockquote class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Ovdje počivaju u miru Božjem<br />
      <strong>OTILIJA i NEDA KOSINA</strong><br />
      <em>čin. Velike župe</em> ✻ 1920. <strong>† 11.11.1943.</strong><br />
      <em>maturantica, <strong>žrtva zračnog napada</strong></em> ✻ 1924. <strong>† 7.IX.1943.</strong><br />
      VLČ. ANTE ✻ 1921 † 2003 — IN MEMORIAM — OSKAR ✻ 1922 † 1954<br />
      <em>Prerano ste rekle zbogom svijete.<br />
      snivajte slatko anđeli moji.<br />
      <strong>Raztužena Majka i Braća.</strong></em></blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>“Here rest in God's peace Otilija and Neda Kosina.
    Otilija, clerk of the Velika župa. Neda, a school-leaver, a victim of an air attack. Too soon
    you said goodbye, O world; sleep sweetly, my angels. The saddened Mother and Brothers.”</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Two words are worth the magnification on their own.</strong>
    <em>Svijete</em> is a <strong>vocative</strong> — the line is spoken to the world, not to the
    girls. And <em>Raztužena</em> is <strong><em>rastužena</em> cut with a z</strong>, the older
    spelling, in a hand that also wrote <em>siečnja</em> and <em>napadaja</em>.</p>
    <p><strong>Read the four names as a sequence and the plaque has been cut three times.</strong>
    <em>Majka i Braća</em> raised it for two sisters in 1943 — so Anka, Ante and Oskar. Then
    <strong>Oskar's own dates</strong> went on it after 1954, and <strong>Ante's</strong> after 2003,
    both under <em>IN MEMORIAM</em>, because neither of them lies there: one died in Buenos Aires
    and the other outlived every person named on the stone he had helped to raise.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The stone says it outright</div>
    <h2>«Nevina žrtva»</h2>
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/line/4505803-pavao-and-anka-kosina-grave.jpg")}
           alt="The Kosina grave at Senj, naming Pavao Kosina as a victim of the air raid" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The Kosina plot, Senj. Three portrait medallions below the inscription.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <blockquote class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">Ovdje počiva u miru Božjem<br />
      <strong>PAVAO KOSINA</strong><br />
      ✳ 15.III.1894. · <strong>pog. 8.X.1943.</strong><br />
      <strong><em>Nevina žrtva zračnog napadaja na Senj</em></strong><br />
      U znak sjećanja podiže — zahvalna supruga i sinovi</blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>“Here rests in God's peace Pavao Kosina, born 15 March
    1894, <em>killed</em> 8 October 1943. An innocent victim of the air raid on Senj. Raised in
    memory by his grateful wife and sons.”</strong></p>
    <p><em>Pog.</em> is <strong><em>poginuo</em></strong> — the word kept for a violent death rather
    than an ordinary one. <strong>The family did not merely record the date. They said what it
    was</strong>, on stone, in a town that had just lost half its buildings.
    <a href={u("/senj-1943/")}>The raid →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">After</div>
    <h2>What the survivors did</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Argentina</h3>
        <p><strong>Oskar Kosina</strong> died at <strong>Buenos Aires on 15 January 1954</strong>,
        aged thirty-one, eleven years after the raid — his printed memorial card gives the day.
        He left a son, <strong>Ivan Christian</strong>. <span class="dek">He may be living, so this archive gives a given name and nothing else.</span></p>
        <p class="dek">The card reads <strong>✻ 4. IV. 1922</strong> — the fourth of
        <em>April</em> — where the tree says <strong>4 September</strong>. The day agrees and the
        month does not, and the two candidates are <strong>IV</strong> and <strong>IX</strong>. A
        card set by a grieving family in Argentina is not a register; neither is a tree. Recorded,
        not resolved.</p>
        <figure class="doc" style="margin-top:1rem">
          <img src={u("/photos/line/9000002-oskar-kosina-memorial-card-1954.jpg")} style="max-width:300px"
               alt="Printed memorial card for Oskar Kosina, 1922-1954" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>His memorial card, printed in Argentina. Supplied already digitally
          enhanced, so the tone is not the card's own.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="dek">It is the second documented destination of this family after
        <a href={u("/port-pirie/")}>Port Pirie</a>, and it fits: of the sixty-nine people in the
        world spelling the name <em>Zubrinic</em>, twenty-nine are in Argentina.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Germany</h3>
        <p><strong>Antun Kosina</strong> (1921–2003) — the eldest surviving son, the one who was
        twenty-two when his father was killed — <strong>became a priest</strong>. His cross reads
        <em><strong>vlč.</strong></em>, <em>velečasni</em>: Reverend. <strong>He has a published
        career, and it is the best-documented life on this page</strong> — see below.</p>
        <figure class="doc" style="margin-top:1rem">
          <img src={u("/photos/line/9000001-antun-kosina-priest-portrait.jpg")} style="max-width:300px"
               alt="Studio portrait of Antun Kosina in a cassock and Roman collar" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>vlč. Ante Kosina. Undated and with no reverse, but the age puts it near his
          ordination — the priesthood as a photograph rather than an abbreviation on a cross.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>He had a daughter, <strong>Paula</strong>. <span class="dek">She may be living, so
        this archive gives a given name and nothing else — no surname, no date, no place. A
        surname and a first name and a country together are an identification, and
        <em>named-bare</em> was always meant to mean enough that the family knows who is meant and
        not enough that a stranger can go looking.</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">1958–1985 · Kaiserslautern, Ludwigshafen, and a good deal of road</div>
    <h2>What he did with the rest of his life</h2>
    <p class="lede">He was <strong>twenty-two</strong> when his father was killed and his two
    sisters died within five weeks. A household of seven became three. <strong>What Ante Kosina
    did next is the best-documented life on this page, and it is not documented by this family —
    it is in the German and Croatian church press, because he spent forty years being useful to
    strangers.</strong></p>

    <blockquote class="note"><em>Na području Ludwigshafena je <strong>1958. iz Rima došao vlč.
      Anton Kosina</strong>, svećenik tadašnje Riječko-senjske nadbiskupije. Vlč. Kosina se
      smjestio na području <strong>Kaiserslauterna</strong>. Tada je u Njemačkoj bilo samo
      <strong>šest hrvatskih svećenika</strong>, pa je pastoralno djelovao u više pokrajina.</em>
      <span class="dek" style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem">«In 1958 vlč. Anton Kosina came to
      the Ludwigshafen area from Rome, a priest of the then Rijeka-Senj archdiocese. He settled
      around Kaiserslautern. There were then only six Croatian priests in Germany, so he worked
      pastorally across several provinces.»</span></blockquote>

    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>Read that number again. Six priests, for the whole of
    West Germany.</strong> He was not given a parish; he was given a share of a country. For nine
    years the job was a car, a case and a Mass wherever Croatians had gathered.</p>

    <p><strong>Then 1967, and everything changed at once.</strong> The first great wave of
    Croatian workers — the <em>Gastarbeiter</em> — arrived in the chemical towns of
    <strong>Ludwigshafen and Mannheim</strong>, <strong>BASF country</strong>, where the
    <em>Wirtschaftswunder</em> ran on other people's hands. He moved to meet them, and founded the
    <strong>Hrvatska katolička misija</strong> there, under the patronage of
    <strong>Blessed Alojzije Stepinac</strong>. <strong>He led it from its founding in 1967 until
    1985 — eighteen years.</strong></p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>And he was a year ahead of it.</strong>
      West Germany and Yugoslavia signed their labour recruitment treaty at
      <strong>Belgrade in 1968</strong> — the first such agreement between a NATO state and a
      non-aligned communist one, and the thing that turned a trickle of Croatian workers into a
      flood. <strong>Kosina founded the mission in 1967.</strong> He had been driving those roads
      for nine years and he could see what was coming before the governments had signed for it.</div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">What that mission actually was</h3>
    <p>It is easy to read «mission» as a building. For the people who came off those trains it
    was <strong>the one room in Germany where the language was theirs</strong> — a Mass they could
    follow, a baptism and a funeral done properly, catechism for children who were growing up
    German, and a priest who could read a German letter for you. <strong>He turned a travelling
    chaplaincy into an institution</strong>, and it outlived him by decades.</p>

    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <tr><th>It seeded others</th><td><em>«Više godina prije osnutka HKM Karlsruhe u tom gradu i
        okolici pastoralno je djelovao <strong>vlč. Anton Kosina iz Ludwigshafena</strong>.»</em>
        <span class="dek" style="display:block">Karlsruhe got its own mission on <strong>3 October
        1970</strong> — on ground he had been working for years. Mannheim, Mosbach and Speyer were
        served the same way before they stood on their own.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>He brought help</th><td><strong>vlč. Marijan Bevanda</strong> (1970–1971) and
        <strong>vlč. Milivoj Galić</strong> (1971–1978), both of the Mostar-Duvno diocese, came to
        assist him as the community outgrew one man.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>He handed it on</th><td>On <strong>1 August 1985</strong> to <strong>fra Ljubo
        Sesar</strong>, who held it for <strong>thirty-one years</strong>, to 2016 — so the thing
        Ante Kosina started has had <strong>three leaders in sixty years</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>It is still there</th><td>About <strong>four thousand</strong> people. It celebrates
        at <strong>St Hedwig in Ludwigshafen</strong>, in the <strong>Franciscan sisters' chapel at
        Kaiserslautern</strong>, at St Joseph in Speyer and at Our Lady of Peace in Homburg —
        <strong>still saying Mass in the town where he began in 1958</strong>.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>And the church in Ludwigshafen is
      dedicated to St Hedwig.</strong> It is a coincidence and nothing more — the parish was there
      long before he was. But the mission that Ante Kosina founded says its Sunday Mass in the
      church of <strong>Sveta Hedviga</strong>, and the first cousin he grew up alongside in Senj,
      the woman this whole archive is built around, was <strong>Hedviga</strong>.</div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>There is a shape to it that is worth saying plainly.</strong>
    A war took his father and his sisters out of one small Adriatic town and scattered what was
    left — <strong>one brother to Buenos Aires, a nephew after him, and Ante himself to Rome</strong>.
    Twenty years later he spent his working life meeting other people's trains: <strong>Croatians
    leaving home for work rather than for their lives, and someone waiting for them who spoke the
    language.</strong> He of all people knew what it was to arrive somewhere and know no one.</p>

    <p><strong>Then he went home.</strong> In <strong>1991</strong>, in his seventies, he was back
    in Senj having his grandmother's grave repaired, and he put his name at the foot of it.
    <strong>He died in 2003</strong> and is named on his sisters' stone under <em>IN MEMORIAM</em>.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">What is documented, and what is only claimed</h3>
    <p>Everything above is from <strong>four published sources</strong> — the Croatian church
    agencies <strong>IKA</strong> and <strong>KTA</strong>, <em>CroExpress</em>, and the
    <strong>HKM Mittelbaden</strong> mission's own history of Karlsruhe — all reachable and all
    quoted in <a href={u("/searched/")}>the register of what has been searched</a>.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Several things now circulating about him are in none of them.</strong>
      That he reached <strong>Rome in 1945</strong>; that he was <strong>ordained in 1948</strong>;
      that his first German years were spent among post-war refugees; that the 1985 handover was a
      transfer from diocesan clergy to the Franciscan Province of Ss Cyril and Methodius; and that
      he died on <strong>3 July 2003</strong>. <strong>Every one of them is plausible and not one
      of them is sourced.</strong> They come from generative search summaries, one of which gave
      his arrival in Germany as 1959 where every real source says <strong>1958</strong> — and gave
      itself away by saying 1958 two paragraphs earlier.
      <p style="margin-top:.7rem;margin-bottom:0"><strong>They are kept, because they are good
      questions.</strong> An ordination year and a death date are exactly what a diocesan archive
      can confirm or deny in one line. <strong>They are not published as facts, and the stone
      gives the year 2003 on its own authority.</strong></p></div>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>The two doors worth knocking on are the
    mission itself and the archdiocese.</strong> HKM Ludwigshafen will hold its own founding
    records and very probably a photograph of him; the <strong>Riječka nadbiskupija</strong> and
    the <strong>Hrvatski dušobrižnički ured</strong> in Frankfurt would each hold an obituary for
    2003 and his ordination year. <strong>A letter to the mission is drafted and has not been
    sent</strong>, and it asks first for a photograph the family may publish.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">9 October 1947 · Buenos Aires, port arrival books</div>
    <h2>Oskar came in on the <em>Philippa</em>, and he was already married</h2>
    <p class="lede">This page said, earlier today, that the Argentine trail was empty.
    <strong>It was empty because the right database had not been searched.</strong></p>
    <blockquote class="note"><strong>KOSINA · OSCAR · 25 · C · YUGOSLAVA/S.H.S. · SEGKA ·
      COLONO · 1947/10/09 · PHILIPPA · GENOVA</strong>
      <span class="dek" style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem">CEMLA — the Buenos Aires port
      arrival books, 4.4 million people, 1882–1960.</span></blockquote>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <tr><th>9 October 1947</th><td><strong>His arrival</strong>, which this archive did not have
        at all. He died <strong>six years and three months later</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Aged 25</th><td>Born about <strong>1922</strong> — agrees with the tree and with his
        memorial card.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>«SEGKA»</th><td><strong>SEGNA — the Italian name for SENJ.</strong> A Buenos Aires
        clerk writing an Italian form of a Croatian town, which is what you get when the man
        boarded at Genoa.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>«COLONO»</th><td><em>Settler</em> — the visa class for post-war agricultural
        immigration, which is how displaced Europeans entered Argentina in 1947.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>From GENOA</th><td>The route out of the Italian displaced-persons camps.
        <strong>Not a voyage from home. An exit.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">«C» — CASADO</th><td><strong>HE WAS ALREADY MARRIED ON THE DAY HE
        LANDED.</strong></td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>That last one changes the work.</strong> This archive has
    been looking for Oskar's wife in Buenos Aires, on the assumption that a man of twenty-five
    arriving alone married there. <strong>He did not.</strong> He married in <strong>Europe</strong>
    — in the camps or in Croatia — and she travelled with him, followed him, or is on the same
    manifest under her maiden name. <strong>So the next document is not an Argentine marriage
    register. It is the passenger list of the <em>Philippa</em>, 9 October 1947.</strong></p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">And he was not the only Kosina on that ship</h3>
    <blockquote class="note"><strong>KOSINA · FRAJNO · 47 · C · CROATA (HRVATSKA) · AGRONOMO ·
      1947/04/01 · PHILIPPA · GENOVA</strong></blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>FRAJNO is FRANJO.</strong> Born about <strong>1900</strong>,
    married, an <strong>agronomist</strong> — <strong>the same ship, the same port, six months
    earlier</strong>. Two men of one surname on one vessel in one year by one exit route is not
    coincidence; it is a family or a network, and probably both.</p>
    <p><strong>Is he a brother?</strong> Martin Kozina and Angjelika Boras had sons in
    <strong>1891</strong> and <strong>1894</strong>, and she died in <strong>1902</strong> — so a
    son born about 1900 <strong>is possible</strong>. It is possible and it is
    <strong>not established</strong>, and he is filed as exactly that in
    <a href={u("/found/")}>the people this tree does not have</a>.</p>
    <p class="dek">One detail of his is worth keeping. His nationality is entered
    <strong>«CROATA (HRVATSKA)»</strong> where every other Yugoslav on these pages is
    «YUGOSLAVA/S.H.S.». <strong>In April 1947 that is a man telling a clerk what he was, and the
    clerk writing it down.</strong></p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">The other rows, and one that had to be checked</h3>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>10 April 1911</th><td>A whole household off the <em>Sofia Hohenberg</em> from
        <strong>Trieste</strong>, all «AUSTRIACO - DALMATA»: <strong>Gerko 30</strong> and
        <strong>Clara 25</strong>, with <strong>Anka 4, Irma 4, Ojeskoslaio 2</strong> and
        <strong>Gelka 0</strong>. <em>Gerko</em> is <strong>Jerko</strong>, <em>Ojeskoslaio</em> is
        <strong>Vjekoslav</strong>, <em>Gelka</em> is <strong>Jelka</strong>; the two four-year-olds
        are twins or an age is wrong. <strong>Croatian names, Adriatic Austrians — and nothing but
        the surname joins them to this family.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>30 November 1928</th><td><strong>Ivan Kosina, 27 and single</strong>, from
        <strong>Hamburg</strong>, born «Velic», a farm labourer. <strong>He is NOT Ivan
        Christian.</strong> Oskar's son was born in Argentina after October 1947; this man was born
        about <strong>1901</strong>. <span class="dek">The names collide and the dates cannot. It
        was checked.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>15 January 1929</th><td>Michal Kosina, 42, <strong>Polish</strong>, from Cherbourg —
        the Kosina of the Subcarpathian village, and no relation.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Twelve rows</th><td><strong>No wife and no son.</strong> She would be under her maiden
        name; he was not born yet. Ivan and Michal each appear twice — CEMLA indexes some arrivals
        from two books.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">The z-spelling was run too, and it is a clean negative</h3>
    <p><strong>KOZINA returns 23 rows and not one of them is this family</strong> — which is worth
    as much as the find, because it tells you which spelling to stop searching.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Bijača</th><td>Spelled <strong>BIJACA, BJOCAR, BEJACA</strong> and <strong>BYCA</strong>
        by four different clerks — a village in <strong>Herzegovina</strong>. Ladislav 1927, Jozo and
        Jure 1928, Bqne and Krunko 1930. <strong>An emigration village, and not ours.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Derventa</th><td>In <strong>Bosnia</strong>. <strong>Lovro 26 and Jela 23 with their
        baby Maria</strong>, arriving together on 11 March 1948 off the <em>Cordoba</em> from Hamburg,
        and <strong>Petar, 70 and a widower</strong>, three months behind them. <span class="dek">The
        baby's birthplace is entered <strong>BAGNOLI</strong> — the displaced-persons camp at Naples —
        and her nationality <em>Italiana</em>. A child born in the camp on the way out.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th>The rest</th><td>Polish (Piotr of Zawoja, Rudolf of Świątoławiec), one Slovene
        (<strong>Franc of Dolenja Vas</strong>, 1930), and two unplaceable. <strong>Nothing touches
        Senj or Kranj.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>And COSINA</th><td>The Spanish phonetic form gives <strong>five rows and not one of
        them is Slavic</strong> — Clemente 1884 and Nicola 1922, both <em>italiana</em> out of Genoa;
        José 1907, <em>española</em>; Nicolina 1908, <em>levantina</em>. <strong>Cosina is an Italian
        and Spanish surname in its own right</strong>, not a mangling of ours. <span class="dek">Which
        is the useful part: the clerks were not turning Kosina into Cosina. They were writing
        Kosina.</span></td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>So the older, Slovene z is not how they were written in
    Argentina.</strong> Oskar went in as <strong>KOSINA</strong>, with the s the family still uses.</p>
    <p><strong>And CAUSINA, the last variant, returns one row</strong> — Ramón Causina, Spanish,
    1905, out of Barcelona. <strong>Which means CEMLA is worked out: four spellings, forty-one
    rows, and exactly one of them is this family.</strong> The database has been asked everything
    it can answer. <strong>Everything further is on the page of the book rather than in the
    index</strong>, and the page has been asked for.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>AND HIS WIFE IS NOT IN THE BOOKS.</strong>
      He landed <strong>married</strong> on 9 October 1947 and <strong>no Kosina woman appears at
      all</strong>, under either spelling. So she did not travel under his name. She is indexed under
      her <strong>maiden name</strong>, or she came by a route these books do not cover — <strong>or
      she did not come.</strong> <strong>That is a question this archive could not even ask
      yesterday.</strong></div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>Four things left, in order of promise.</strong>
      <strong>1.</strong> The <strong>manifest of the <em>Philippa</em>, 9 October 1947</strong> —
      his wife is on it. <strong>2.</strong> <strong>KOZINA, COSINA, CAUSINA</strong> run through
      CEMLA as well; only KOSINA has been. <strong>3.</strong> <strong>The reverse of his memorial
      card</strong>, never scanned, which on a card of that date almost certainly reads
      <em>«Ožalošćena obitelj: supruga … sin …»</em> — <strong>the widow's name</strong>.
      <strong>4.</strong> The <strong>Croatian chaplaincy in Buenos Aires</strong>, where fra
      Leonard Rusković served from 1929 until his death in 1958 and therefore buried him.</div>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>CEMLA is free and behind a CAPTCHA</strong>,
    which this archive will not answer, so the search was run by hand by the account holder.
    <a href={u("/searched/")}>Everything tried, with what it gave →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">1991 · forty-eight years later</div>
    <h2>And then a Kosina went back and mended a grave</h2>
    <p class="lede">In the Senj cemetery stands the stone of
    <strong>Tereza Žubrinić</strong> (1867–1931) — <strong>Hedviga's grandmother</strong>, and
    <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>one of the sixteen people on this archive's direct line</a>. She
    was Anka's mother, so she was <strong>Antun Kosina's grandmother too</strong>.</p>
    <blockquote class="note">OVDJE POČIVAJU U MIRU BOŽJEM …<br />
      <span class="dek">and at the foot:</span><br />
      <strong>OBNOVIO POP ANTE KOSINA ~ 1991</strong></blockquote>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>“Restored by Fr. Ante Kosina, 1991.”</strong></p>
    <p>The son of the man killed in 1943 — by then a priest in his seventies — <strong>went back and
    had his grandmother's stone repaired</strong>, and put his name on it as the man who did it.
    <strong>That line is the only reason the name Kosina was anywhere in this archive's own
    records</strong> before today: a footnote on somebody else's grave, read off a photograph in
    October 2024.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What is not known, and is worth asking for</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th class="warn">ANSWERED</th><td><strong>Martin's father.</strong> This row used to say
        that Senj baptisms are filmed from 1734 and nobody had looked. <strong>Martin was not
        baptised in Senj.</strong> He was baptised at Kranj–Šmartin on 16 November 1841, and the
        register puts the tick in <em>unehelich</em> and leaves the <em>Vater</em> column blank.
        <strong>There is no father to find in a baptismal register</strong> — only, possibly, in a
        paternity acknowledgement or a later marriage, and neither has surfaced.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Maria Dortschen's death</th><td>The last of the six Carniolans without one, and the
        parish indexes for <strong>1812–1835</strong> and <strong>1836–1869</strong> have both been
        read end to end. <strong>She is in neither.</strong> Next: the death registers of
        <strong>1870–1890</strong> and <strong>1890–1911</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn">ANSWERED</th><td><strong>Otilija died 11 November 1943</strong> and
        <strong>Neda was the air-raid victim</strong>, 7 September. Both off the family's own
        gravestone, sent 21 September 2026. This row asked which of them died with their father:
        <strong>neither did</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Which raid killed Neda</th><td>Her stone says <em>žrtva zračnog napada</em> on
        <strong>7 September 1943</strong>, a month before the one that killed Pavao. <strong>How
        many times Senj was bombed that autumn</strong> is now the open question, and this archive
        has been writing as though there were one.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>The elder Antun</th><td>Antonius Joannes Kosina (1891–1925) married
        <strong>Anka Kasunić</strong>, who is a name and nothing else here. He died at
        <strong>thirty-four</strong>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Oskar's wife</th><td><strong>Unnamed in the tree</strong>, though their son is in it.
        Buenos Aires, before 1954.</td></tr>
    </table>
